<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eşrefoğlu Mosque</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Eşrefoğlu Süleyman Bey Mosque</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eşrefoğlu Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beysehir">Beyşehir</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, was built in 1296–1299 by Süleyman Bey of the Eshrefid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>. Its prayer hall has a forest of carved cedar columns supporting a flat wooden roof, with painted ceilings, tiled mihrab and carved fittings, and it is among the finest surviving wooden mosques. It is part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage listing of wooden hypostyle mosques of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/esrefoglu-mosque.jpg" alt="Eşrefoğlu Mosque" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beysehir_Eshrefoglu_Camii_4369.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
